  <body>In 1940, the Wehrmact issued a specification for a semi-automatic rifle. Both Mauser and Walther submitted prototypes — the Gewehr 41(M) and Gewehr 41(W), respectively. The Mauser rifle followed the specification much more closely — the specification called for no moving parts on the surface and a bolt action in case the auto-loading mechanism failed — but ultimately the Walther rifle was a far better weapon (more than partially because it had ignored those parts of the specification) and ended up being adopted by the Germany army.

The Gewehr 41's most obvious drawback is its reloading: Much like the Lee-Enfield, it uses two five-round stripper clips. This allowed it to use the same clip as the Kar98k, but it was much slower to reload than weapons with detachable magazines such as the M1 Garand. A second, less obvious problem was it was prone to mechanical problems due to the complex muzzle trap system that, again, was a result of the Wehrmact's original specification. Both of these problems were corrected for the Gewehr 43.</body>
